<section class="faq section-white">
  <div class="container faq-layout">
    <div class="faq-intro">
      <div class="section-header">
        <span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span>
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p>Quick answers about HR in the Netherlands.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-cta">
        <span>Still have a question?</span>
        <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-secondary">Contact us →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How long does it take to hire an employee in the Netherlands?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Typically 2-4 weeks for the administrative setup, though this varies based on the employee's nationality and visa requirements. For EU citizens, the process is faster. For non-EU citizens requiring a Highly Skilled Migrant visa, allow 4-6 weeks.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What is the 30% ruling and does my company qualify?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>The 30% ruling is a Dutch tax advantage for incoming expat employees, allowing 30% of their salary to be paid tax-free. To qualify, the employee must be recruited from abroad, earn more than the minimum salary set for the ruling each year, and possess specific expertise not readily available in the Netherlands.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Do I need a Dutch entity to hire employees?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Not necessarily. A Dutch B.V. is the most common route, but a foreign company can also employ staff in the Netherlands through a registered Dutch branch, or hire without its own entity through an Employer of Record (EOR) or a payroll provider.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What are the mandatory employer costs in the Netherlands?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Beyond gross salary, which must be at least the statutory minimum wage, expect approximately 20-30% additional costs: social insurance contributions (~15-20%), 8% holiday allowance (vakantiegeld), sick leave insurance and, where applicable, pension contributions. A pension scheme is only mandatory if a sector pension fund or a collective labour agreement (CAO) applies to your company; otherwise it is not legally required, though it is common.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How does sick leave work under Dutch law?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Dutch employers must continue paying at least 70% of an employee's salary during illness for up to 2 years (104 weeks). During long-term sick leave, you're also required to follow the Wet Poortwachter reintegration process.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Can you help with urgent dismissal situations?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Yes, that's our HR S.O.S. service. Dutch dismissal law is complex and heavily favors employees. We provide guidance on the same business day and complete legal support throughout the process.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

